WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you for being here fo
one of our live shows.

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:03.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
-A live show.
It's an amazing concept.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.338 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Have you ever considered
doing your show live?

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:07.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughs ]
I was joking backstage.

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like,
"This is a big deal, live.

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:10.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-We do this every night.

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:12.345 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, you do it
every single night.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.481 align:start size:94% position:6%
Every single night.
You turn it around really fast

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:16.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
Tonight was
a little strange for you,

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:17.584 line:92%
because you were doing your show

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:18.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
during the first hour
of the debate.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's so weird that we've ha
this two nights in a row.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.858
I mean, the first time, we had
the debates on MSNBC, right?

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:28.194
So, these ones, we did not have.
They were only on CNN.

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're only on the networ
that gets them.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:31.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you're in that
segment meeting.

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:33.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like, "Should we not
mention the debate

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
because maybe on the off chanc
we remind someone

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's happening
and they turn over there?"

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.873
-Yeah.
-That's kind of dumb. They know.

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:42.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Because I feel like the peopl
who watch your show

00:00:42.409 --> 00:00:44.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
are obviously invested
in politics,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
so it seems like
a very small slice of people

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:47.480 size:75% position:38%
that would be, like,
invested in politics

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
but also not
care about the debates.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:52.352 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, thank you.
-Yeah.

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:53.386 line:92%
-I like to think of the people

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
invested in politics
invested in me.

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:56.122 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah, that's true.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.558
-Which is a small number,
as the ratings showed.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:01.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But, obviously,
you managed to go back.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:04.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
You watched the debate.
A lot about healthcare tonight

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:06.733 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes.
-Substantive, I think.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
Sometimes, I would imagine
for most people,

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:10.403 size:88% position:44% line:92%
a little hard to follow.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:12.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like the candidate
do a good job

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
laying out what their
differences are

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:15.742 line:92%
in regards to healthcare?

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:19.145
-I thought the first night
was actually sharper on this

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the access
was very clear.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:23.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was two people were
advocating Medicare for all.

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
Single payer.
Everyone enrolls in one plan

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
There will be no
private plans anymore.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:28.922 size:81% position:41% line:92%
We'll transition that.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then other people
attacking that.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:31.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I actually
think Medicare for all --

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:33.860
Whatever you think about its
popularity or workability,

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.296 size:75% position:38%
at least it is clear
what it is, right?

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:38.198
So, people can say, "Well, how
are you going to pay for it?

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:39.699 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I don't know if I
want to give up

00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:40.733 line:92%
my private health insurance.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:43.403 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the sort of
lines of debate were clear

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:46.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
Tonight, with these more kin
of, like, half-measures

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.775
about the public option --
You'll have a $1,000 deductible,

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
and 8.6% of your income,
but not if you're --

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, that gets
way, way, way harder to follow

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.950
-And then it becomes a bit --
feel as though the takeaway is

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
who's making the most effectiv
attack on someone else?

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:04.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
Tonight definitely seemed like
a night of attacking.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:05.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
Joe Biden is obviously
the front-runner.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's going to
take those attacks.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:10.363
It seemed like, last night
they were making Delaney

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:14.334
the stand-in for Joe Biden
because he was sort of the

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.301 size:84% position:42% line:92%
moderate who was there.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:18.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
And despite polling at 1%,
he sort of had a starring role

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:20.907 size:78% position:39%
-It was really funny,
because, obviously,

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:23.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
the thing that
we haven't seen yet is --

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:24.344 line:92%
you want to see a stage that has

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:28.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
Harris, Buttigieg, Biden,
Warren, and Sanders together

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:31.518 align:start size:94% position:6%
Those are the people who are
polling in the top five.

00:02:31.518 --> 00:02:32.385 size:88% position:44% line:92%
And we haven't had that.

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, last night,
I think what CNN tried to do -

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's not a
crazy approach to this --

00:02:35.922 --> 00:02:37.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
is they were like,
"Let's just make someone

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:40.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
a Biden stand-in, because we
don't have Biden here."

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they were like,
"Who's the guy at the end?

00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:45.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
Delaney. Let's do him."
-Yeah.

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:47.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was like Brad Pitt
to Leonardo in

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.801 line:92%
"Once Upon a Time in Hollywood."

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:53.506
-Yeah. I haven't seen it yet
-It's a stuntman joke, yeah.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.408 line:92%
Not a lot of people have.

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:58.745
And then I want to ask about
the issue of electability.

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:01.915
Obviously, this is a questio
that's coming out right now.

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:03.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one thing
that people ask is,

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.352
"Is this good for people
to be attacking everyone?"

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.321 align:start size:94% position:6%
Does that weaken a candidate
when they go

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:08.988 size:88% position:44% line:92%
to the general election?

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
You could argue, certainly
in the last election,

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:13.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hillary Clinton
didn't have to go through

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:16.462 size:84% position:42%
this sort of trial by a
bunch of candidates,

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:19.332
maybe a little bit with Bernie
but it certainly wasn't this

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:20.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like it's
good or bad

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:24.637
for whoever emerges from this to
have been sort of attacked by,

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:26.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
at one point, you know,
22 different other candidates?

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.642
-You know, I don't really know
that I know the answer to that

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:31.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, in some ways,
it's unavoidable,

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:35.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
so who's to say
whether it's good or bad?

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:36.049 size:78% position:39% line:92%
It's going to happen.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:38.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you know,
politics is about conflict

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:39.552 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think that what
I would say about

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.955 align:start size:94% position:6%
the first four debates in th
Democratic Party is --

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:42.956 line:92%
they have been substantive

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:45.525 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, people do disagree about
whether we should have

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:46.559 line:92%
a single-payer system or not

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are arguments
on both sides

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are not, like,
you know, trivial.

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:51.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are real arguments
on both sides,

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:52.966 line:92%
and those need to be hashed out.

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:56.336
People disagree about whether we
should decriminalize

00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:58.037 size:78% position:39%
unauthorized entry to
the United States,

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:00.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is currently
a criminal misdemeanor

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:01.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
but could be handled as
a civil matter.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are people on
different sides of that.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:05.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's good to fight about
that stuff.

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, it's good
to fight about that stuff

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it is inevitable
to fight about that stuff.

